        "marks": 28,
        "model": "Functions of carbohydrates in the body: (1) Primary energy source — glucose from carbohydrate digestion is the body's preferred fuel, providing 4 kcal per gram; it is essential for brain and nervous system function. (2) Protein sparing — when carbohydrate intake is adequate, protein is not used for energy and can be reserved for growth and repair. (3) Dietary fibre aids gut motility — insoluble fibre speeds the passage of food through the intestine, preventing constipation and reducing risk of bowel disease. Sources in the diet: Simple carbohydrates — sugar, honey, fruit, milk (lactose). Complex carbohydrates (starch) — bread, potatoes, rice, pasta, cereals. Dietary fibre — wholegrain cereals, vegetables, pulses, fruit. Properties of carbohydrates: (1) Solubility — simple sugars dissolve readily in water; starch does not dissolve in cold water but forms a colloidal suspension. (2) Gelatinisation — starch granules absorb water on heating and swell to form a gel, thickening liquids (e.g. custard, white sauce). (3) Caramelisation — sugars heated in dry heat above 160°C break down to form brown pigments with a characteristic flavour (e.g. toffee, caramel sauce). (4) Maillard reaction — sugars react with proteins under heat to produce brown colour and flavour (e.g. bread crust, biscuits). (5) Fermentation — yeast converts sugars to carbon dioxide and alcohol; CO2 causes dough to rise in bread-making.",
