        "marks": 12,
        "model": "Three benefits of sourcing consumer information before purchasing an appliance: (1) Ensures the appliance meets the consumer's specific needs — researching before purchase allows the consumer to compare specifications (capacity, programmes, energy rating) across different models and brands, ensuring the chosen appliance is suitable for the household's size and usage patterns. For example, a family of six requires a larger capacity washing machine than a single person; reading reviews highlights whether a product performs as advertised. (2) Enables comparison of price and value for money — gathering information from multiple retailers and online sources allows the consumer to identify the best price, taking into account running costs (energy consumption over the appliance's lifetime) as well as purchase price. An A-rated appliance may cost more initially but saves money on electricity bills over several years. (3) Protects the consumer from poor quality or unsafe products — consulting independent consumer organisations such as the Competition and Consumer Protection Commission (CCPC) or Which? magazine reveals safety issues, recall notices and common faults; this prevents the consumer from purchasing an unreliable or dangerous product and helps them enforce their statutory rights under the Sale of Goods and Supply of Services Act 1980 if problems arise.",
