        "model": "Study of a household appliance with a motor — the food processor: Type of appliance: The food processor is a countertop small electrical appliance with a motor housed in the base unit. It is used in food preparation to chop, slice, grate, blend, whisk and knead, replacing manual labour. Factors to be considered when selecting: (1) Power/wattage — a motor of at least 600–800W is required for effective performance; a more powerful motor handles harder tasks such as kneading bread dough or processing raw root vegetables. (2) Capacity of bowl — the size of the work bowl (typically 1.5–3 litres) should match household size; a larger bowl reduces the need for multiple batches. (3) Range of attachments — a wide variety of interchangeable blades and discs (slicing disc, grating disc, dough hook, citrus juicer) increases versatility and value for money. (4) Safety features — a locking lid that prevents operation unless securely fitted, non-slip feet, and blade guards are essential safety considerations. (5) Ease of cleaning — dishwasher-safe components and smooth surfaces without crevices are important for hygiene. Design of the appliance: A well-designed food processor has a stable, heavy base to prevent movement during use; a transparent work bowl allows monitoring of progress; control settings (pulse, low, high) give flexibility; a feed tube in the lid allows ingredients to be added safely while the machine is running. Guidelines for use: Always ensure the bowl and blade are locked in position before switching on; never put hands near moving blades; unplug before assembling or removing attachments; do not exceed the maximum fill line; clean all parts thoroughly after use to prevent cross-contamination.",
